    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder Rankings",
    tags: [
      "best pumpkin spice protein powder",
      "pumpkin spice protein powder",
      "pumpkin pie protein powder",
      "pumpkin spice latte protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Pumpkin Spice Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Six pumpkin spice and pumpkin pie powders listed on Amazon this fall, checked against the label for that exact flavor, with the sweeteners, the seasonal catch and the cost per 25 g of protein called out. Plus the vanilla-and-purée version you can make all year.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#compared">Compared at a Glance</a>
      <a href="#seasonal">The Seasonal Catch</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#what-makes-it-pumpkin">What Makes It Taste Like Pumpkin</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Sugar</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">What Buyers Say</a>
      <a href="#diy">The DIY Version</a>
      <a href="#recipes">Lattes, Oats and Baking</a>
      <a href="#not-ranked">Flavors We Did Not Rank</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best pumpkin spice protein powder for most people is PEScience Select Protein in Pumpkin Pie: per the label at the time of writing it gives 24 g of protein in about 120 calories per 31 g scoop, from a whey and casein blend that thickens like pie filling and bakes well. RYSE Loaded in Little Debbie Pumpkin Spice Rolls is the sweeter, dessert-style pick, Nutricost's Pumpkin Spice Whey Isolate packs the most protein per scoop, and Naked Shake, Truvani and Orgain cover dairy-free buyers. Almost every pumpkin flavor is seasonal, so stock comes and goes. If yours sells out, a good vanilla powder, a spoon of pumpkin purée and pumpkin pie spice gets you a shake that is arguably closer to real pumpkin than any tub. This page is about the flavor; if you want protein made from pumpkin seeds, see our separate <a href="/best-pumpkin-seed-protein-powder/">best pumpkin seed protein powder</a> guide.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Pumpkin Spice Protein Powders Compared at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            All six picks side by side. Protein and calories are per labeled serving, which is one scoop for the whey powders and two scoops for Naked Shake and Orgain. Every figure is per the label or a retailer copy of the label at the time of writing, varies by flavor and can change between seasonal runs, so check the panel on the tub you actually receive. Where a number came from a nutrition database rather than the brand, we say so in the product card.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweeteners</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PEScience Select Pumpkin Pie</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.54</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">RYSE Loaded Pumpkin Spice Rolls</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.85</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Whey Isolate Pumpkin Spice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies widely by listing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Shake Pumpkin Spice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cane sugar (about 3-4 g)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.75</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truvani Pumpkin Spice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monk fruit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.12 at list</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Vegan Pumpkin Spice</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check panel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Erythritol, stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.14</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The split that matters most is protein base. PEScience, RYSE and Nutricost are dairy powders, and they deliver 24 to 30 g of protein per scoop with almost no sugar. Naked Shake, Truvani and Orgain are plant blends built on pea protein, at 20 to 21 g per serving. If you drink dairy without trouble, the whey options give you more protein for the calories and a creamier, more pie-like texture in milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second split is sweetener. The three dairy picks all use sucralose and acesulfame potassium, the standard pairing for low-sugar sports powders. The plant picks go the other way: Naked Shake uses a few grams of cane sugar, Truvani uses monk fruit, and Orgain uses erythritol and stevia. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, that narrows this list to the plant tubs or the DIY route further down this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            Costs are worked out from the prices we could see in November 2026, which for several tubs were brand or retailer list prices rather than Amazon prices, because seasonal Amazon listings swing a lot from week to week. Treat the column as a ranking rather than a quote. The cost section below shows the math, so you can rerun it in a few seconds with whatever price your Amazon listing shows on the day you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seasonal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Seasonal Catch: Why Pumpkin Tubs Disappear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pumpkin is the most seasonal flavor in protein powder. PEScience first launched Pumpkin Pie Select as a limited edition in fall 2021 and has brought it back in several later autumns, including 2025, according to supplement news coverage of each release. RYSE describes its Little Debbie Pumpkin Spice Rolls flavor as a special edition fall release. Naked Nutrition calls its Pumpkin Spice Naked Shake seasonal and available while supplies last, and Orgain has described its pumpkin spice powder as a limited-time return.
          </p>

          <p>
            That pattern has practical consequences. Most pumpkin tubs appear between late August and October and thin out by winter. Once a brand's own stock runs low, Amazon listings often switch to third-party sellers, and prices on those listings can climb well above the original list price. A tub that cost about $40 in September can show up at a noticeably higher price in January from a seller you have never heard of.
          </p>

          <p>
            The safest way to buy is from a listing that says ships from and sold by Amazon, or from the brand's own Amazon storefront. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">guide to buying protein powder on Amazon</a> explains how to check the seller line, when Subscribe and Save is worth it, and how to avoid counterfeit or tampered tubs from third-party sellers. Check the best-by date when it arrives, especially on any tub bought after the season ends.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you love the flavor, buying two tubs in October is reasonable, since unopened whey powders usually carry a best-by date well over a year out. Do not buy more than you will finish, though. Ground spices lose aroma over time once exposed to air, which is true in a spice jar and likely in an opened tub too, so keep the lid tight and store the tub somewhere cool and dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other way around the seasonal problem is to stop depending on it. A year-round vanilla plus real pumpkin and spice covers the gap when your favorite tub is gone, and it is the approach brands themselves use in their recipes. Dymatize, for example, publishes pumpkin protein recipes built on its Gourmet Vanilla ISO100 with pumpkin purée and pumpkin pie spice rather than a pumpkin-flavored powder. The DIY section below gives the ratios.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Pumpkin Spice Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked by label quality for the pumpkin spice or pumpkin pie flavor specifically, then by protein per serving, sweetener and cost per 25 g of protein. Nutrition figures are per the label or a retailer copy of it at the time of writing and vary by flavor and seasonal run; prices are those we saw in November 2026 and move often. Most of these flavors are seasonal and may sell out. We have not taste-tested these powders; taste notes are buyer feedback reported as such.
          </p>

