          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best pumpkin spice protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, PEScience Select Protein in Pumpkin Pie. Per the label it gives 24 g of protein in about 120 calories per scoop from a whey and casein blend that thickens well and bakes well. RYSE's Little Debbie flavor is sweeter, Nutricost's isolate has the most protein per scoop, and Naked Shake is the best dairy-free option. All are seasonal.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin spice protein powder available all year?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually not. Most pumpkin flavors, including PEScience Pumpkin Pie, RYSE Pumpkin Spice Rolls, Naked Shake Pumpkin Spice and Orgain Pumpkin Spice, are described by their brands as seasonal or limited. They tend to appear in late summer and thin out by winter. Outside the season, a vanilla powder with pumpkin purée and pumpkin pie spice is the most reliable substitute.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does pumpkin spice protein powder contain real pumpkin?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most do not. Pumpkin spice flavor comes mainly from cinnamon, nutmeg, ginger and cloves, plus natural flavors. Nutricost's Pumpkin Spice Isolate, per retailer copies of its label, lists cinnamon and natural flavors but no pumpkin. Truvani says its pumpkin note comes from organic pumpkin seed powder. For real pumpkin, add a quarter cup of plain purée to a blended shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How do I make a pumpkin spice protein shake with vanilla powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Blend one scoop of vanilla protein, a quarter cup of plain canned pumpkin purée, half a teaspoon of pumpkin pie spice, 8 to 10 oz of milk and a handful of ice for about 30 seconds. Add a teaspoon of maple syrup if your vanilla is lightly sweetened, and a pinch of salt to sharpen the spice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I put pumpkin spice protein powder in coffee?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, with care. Very hot coffee can make whey clump, so mix the powder with a few ounces of cold milk first, then pour in the coffee, or let the coffee cool slightly. Iced lattes are easier, since cold liquid dissolves whey isolates cleanly. A handheld frother helps either way and takes only a few seconds.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there a pumpkin spice protein powder without artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Naked Shake Pumpkin Spice uses cane sugar, Truvani Pumpkin Spice uses monk fruit, and Orgain uses erythritol and stevia, per their labels at the time of writing. The whey picks on this list use sucralose and acesulfame potassium. For full control, make a DIY shake from an unsweetened vanilla, purée, spice and a little maple syrup.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin spice protein powder the same as pumpkin seed protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Pumpkin spice protein powder is a flavor, usually whey or pea protein flavored with cinnamon and other spices. Pumpkin seed protein is a plant protein made from pressed pumpkin seeds, typically sold unflavored with a green tint and nutty taste. Our separate pumpkin seed protein powder guide covers that product for people who want a pea-free plant protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

